# XSH cnd_signal(), cnd_broadcast()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Waiters wait once with no predicate: a spurious wakeup can only make
# the wake-up checks pass early, never fail them. The signal is sent
# only once every waiter is inside its wait (it holds the mutex the
# waiters released there).

assertion  XSH.cnd_signal.001
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       With no thread blocked on the condition variable, cnd_signal() and
           cnd_broadcast() have no effect and return thrd_success (the mutex
           held or not).
test       t/001-no-waiters.c

assertion  XSH.cnd_signal.002
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       With three threads blocked in cnd_wait() on one condition
           variable, a single cnd_signal() (called without owning the mutex)
           returns thrd_success and at least one of them returns, with
           thrd_success, within 5 s.
note       The other waiters are released afterwards by cnd_broadcast().
test       t/002-signal-wakes.c

assertion  XSH.cnd_signal.003
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       With four threads blocked in cnd_wait() on one condition variable,
           one cnd_broadcast() returns thrd_success and all four return from
           cnd_wait() with thrd_success within 5 s.
test       t/003-broadcast-wakes.c

assertion  XSH.cnd_signal.004
anchor     functions/cnd_broadcast.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A thread unblocked from cnd_wait() by cnd_signal() (called with
           the mutex held) returns owning the mutex: while it keeps it for
           300 ms, the signaller's mtx_trylock() returns thrd_busy.
test       t/004-woken-owns-mutex.c
